let __v_5099 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"plumber\",\"A plumber's day is a list of jobs, not one job: a dripping tap, a blocked loo, a boiler that will not fire, a bathroom that has to be piped and then signed off. Most of the work is diagnosis under a sink or in a loft, and the good plumbers earn their money on the second call-out to the same house. It is dirty and it is on your knees, and the money is in gas and heating rather than taps.\"],[\"electrician\",\"An electrician's day is part first fix, part fault-finding: chasing walls and pulling cable on a new build, then testing and certifying, or tracing why one circuit keeps tripping in a house that has been added to four times. Every finished job ends with a certificate, so the paperwork is part of the trade. It is cleaner than plumbing and more cramped - lofts, cupboards and floorboards.\"],[\"mechanic\",\"A mechanic's day is servicing and diagnosing: oil and filters on a routine service, then brakes, clutches and suspension on older cars, and an hour or two chasing a warning light that could be a sensor or a wiring fault. Fault codes point at systems, not parts, so the skill is testing rather than guessing, and the job is standing, lifting and lying under a car in all weathers.\"],[\"bricklayer\",\"A bricklayer's day is blocks and bricks to a line: setting out the corners, running a course up, keeping it plumb and level, and leaving the openings where the drawings say. It is the fastest visible trade on a site and the hardest on the back and shoulders, and cold weather stops mortar going off. Piecework rates make a good bricklayer's pay look high until you count the days lost to rain.\"],[\"carpenter\",\"A carpenter's day is either second fix in a finished house - doors, skirting, stairs, kitchens - or a workshop bench making what goes in. Site carpenters do more cutting and fitting, bench joiners do more setting out and jointing, and both need to read a drawing and work to a millimetre. Dust, noise and knees, and the good ones are never out of work.\"],[\"plasterer\",\"A plasterer's day is walls and ceilings: boarding, then skim, then the corners and the trowel work, and the finish is judged by whether a light shows a ripple across it. It is wet, heavy and fast - a ceiling has to be got right in one go before it sets - and the shoulders and wrists take the wear.\"],[\"painter and decorator\",\"A painter's day is preparation, and the painting is the easy part: filling, sanding, caulking, masking, then two coats and a careful line where the wall meets the ceiling. It is quiet, indoors and tidy work, and the difference between a good job and a bad one is nearly all in what happened before the paint went on.\"],[\"roofer\",\"A roofer's day is up a ladder or on a roof: replacing slipped tiles, fixing a leak that has been chased around a ceiling for months, felting and battening a new roof, or clearing gutters. It is the most weather-dependent trade there is, and the one where the work is done at height in the cold, so head for a roofer with insurance and a written quote.\"],[\"builder\",\"A builder's day is the whole site rather than one trade: ordering materials, setting out, keeping the trades in the right order, checking the work, and being the person the customer rings. The tools come out less than people expect and the phone never stops. Small jobs are one person and a van, and a house extension is a month of juggling.\"],[\"hgv driver\",\"A lorry driver's day is mostly waiting and then concentrating hard: loading at a depot, driving to a timed slot, waiting to be tipped, then the next one. The law allows 9 hours of driving a day, 10 twice a week, with a 45-minute break after four and a half hours, and tachograph rules are enforced. Nights out and early starts are normal, and the pay is in the hours and the overtime.\"],[\"delivery driver\",\"A delivery driver's day is a route of 80 to 150 drops and a van: load in the morning, then drive, park, carry, knock, repeat, with the phone tracking you all day. The skill is knowing where you can legally stop and how long each drop really takes. It is physical, the weather is the office, and the day is usually over when the van is empty.\"],[\"bus driver\",\"A bus driver's day is a rota of short runs with a break in the middle: the same route, the same stops, and traffic that moves the timetable around. The driving is the easy part and the passengers are the job - fares, buggies, wheelchairs, and the last bus on a Saturday night. Shifts are split, so an early start and a late finish are the same day.\"],[\"taxi driver\",\"A taxi driver's day is the waiting as much as the driving: ranks, bookings, airport runs and the school contract if you can get one. The money is in knowing when the town is busy, and the cost is in the car and the licence, which come out of the takings. Evenings and weekends are the work, so it suits people who do not want office hours.\"],[\"farmer\",\"A farmer's day follows the animals and the season: milking twice a day every day, or moving stock, checking fences and water, then the field work in its window - drilling, spraying, silage, harvest. There is no weekend in livestock, and harvest means long days while the weather holds. The income is lumpy, and a lot of it comes from subsidy and contracting as much as from the produce.\"],[\"nurse\",\"A nurse's day on a ward is a handover, then a list of patients with their medicines, observations, washes, dressings and notes, and a doctor's round somewhere in the middle. Shifts are 12 hours, three or four a week, days and nights, and the last hour is usually the busiest. The clinical work is a third of it; the rest is families, paperwork and keeping a dozen things moving at once.\"],[\"care worker\",\"A care worker's day is a round of visits or a shift in a home: getting people up, washed, dressed and fed, medicines, moving and handling, and writing down what happened. Home care is often 15-minute slots with travel between them and no paid travel time at some employers. It is personal work done at speed, and the good ones know the person as well as the task.\"],[\"midwife\",\"A midwife's day is either antenatal clinic, postnatal checks on the ward, or labour ward looking after one woman through her labour and writing the record as it happens. Shifts are 12 hours, days and nights, and labour ward is unpredictable, so a quiet night can turn into a section and a transfer in an hour. It is the most hands-on job in the hospital and the one with the most joy in it.\"],[\"paramedic\",\"A paramedic's day is a series of 999 calls, each one starting with almost no information: chest pain, a fall, a mental health crisis, a road collision. You assess, treat within your scope, decide who needs hospital and who can stay at home, and write it up between jobs. Shifts are 12 hours and breaks are whatever the calls allow.\"],[\"police officer\",\"A police officer's day is calls and paperwork: attending incidents, taking statements, arresting and processing, then a file that has to stand up in court. Response teams work shifts over 24 hours, and the honest description is long stretches of tedium with short periods of real danger. Most of the job is talking to people who are having their worst day.\"],[\"firefighter\",\"A firefighter's day is drills, checks and training around the shouts: testing kit, learning building layouts, fitness, and community visits, with the crew cooking and eating together on a 24-hour shift. Fires are a small part of the calls - more are road collisions, floods, and helping an ambulance crew get a heavy person downstairs.\"],[\"teacher\",\"A teacher's day is teaching five or six lessons, plus the parts nobody sees: planning, marking, data, parents' evening, and the break duty. Directed hours are about 1,265 a year over 195 days, and the planning and marking on top make 45 to 55 hours in term time. The classroom itself is the good part, and the holidays are the trade for the term-time hours.\"],[\"teaching assistant\",\"A teaching assistant's day is support in the classroom: helping the children who are stuck, running small groups for reading or phonics, and managing behaviour alongside the teacher. The hours are term-time only and usually finish with the children, so it fits round a family, and the pay is hourly rather than a salary. The work is with the same children all year.\"],[\"social worker\",\"A social worker's day is visits, assessments and the phone: seeing a family, writing up the visit, chasing a school or a housing officer, and a meeting about a child or an adult at risk. The caseload is the job, and the paperwork is the reason people leave. It is the work where one decision can change a family's year, so supervision and recording are taken seriously.\"],[\"train driver\",\"A train driver's day is a diagram of trips: sign on, check the unit, drive the same route with the same stops, and hand over at the other end. A computer does much of the driving, so the job is watching the line, the signals and the speed, and taking over when it does not. Shifts are unsocial and start early, and a driver's year is about 35 hours a week with rest days in the roster.\"],[\"barrister\",\"A barrister's day is either in court or in chambers preparing for it: reading the papers, drafting the questions, then standing up and doing the case, and afterwards the note on what the judge said. The self-employed model is the norm, so fees, chambers rent and the VAT are yours to run.\"],[\"architect\",\"An architect's day is drawings and coordination: developing a design, meeting the engineer and the client, checking the building regulations, and answering site queries as it is built. A project runs for years, so most days are a step on something that will not be finished for a long time.\"],[\"security guard\",\"A security guard's day is a patrol, a post or a control room: walking the building, checking passes, watching screens, and writing the log. Shifts cover 24 hours and nights are the norm, and most of the job is being alert for a long time and being calm when something happens.\"],[\"office worker\",\"An office worker's day is a list of tasks and meetings: email, a spreadsheet, a document, a call, and the meeting that interrupts all of it. The hours are usually 9 to 5 with 37.5 or 40 on paper, and the skill is holding a deadline while people ask for things. It is quiet work done sitting down.\"],[\"factory worker\",\"A factory worker's day is a station on a line: the same operation, at the pace the line sets, for a shift, with a break in the middle. Shifts run round the clock and often rotate, the noise needs ear protection, and the pay is a rate per hour plus shift allowance.\"],[\"pharmacist\",\"A pharmacist's day is prescriptions: checking what the doctor has written, dispensing, answering the counter, and giving advice on a cough or a rash. The clinical part is spotting a dose that is wrong or a pair of medicines that should not mix, and the shop work is the rest.\"],[\"librarian\",\"A librarian's day is the desk, the stock and the enquiries: helping people find things, ordering and cataloguing, running sessions for children or for a reading group, and keeping the computers working. The quiet is a myth - it is public-facing work with a research job attached.\"],[\"doctor\",\"A doctor's day is a clinic list or a ward round: ten-minute appointments in general practice, or a hospital round with the notes and the tests for each patient, then the jobs it produces - referrals, prescriptions, a call to a family. GPs carry a list of 30 or more appointments a day and the hospital work is shifts. Most of it is pattern-spotting and explaining, and the pressure is the clock.\"],[\"dentist\",\"A dentist's day is a list of 15 to 30 minute appointments: check-ups, fillings, and the emergencies squeezed in at the end of the morning. Most patients need a clean, a filling or reassurance, and the work is done sitting down in a lit box with an assistant. The practice is a business, so the diary and the recall list matter as much as the drill.\"],[\"vet\",\"A vet's day is a morning of consultations - vaccinations, skin, limps, a cat that will not eat - and an afternoon of operations, dentals and x-rays. Large animal vets are out on farms instead, and out-of-hours cover is shared between practices. It is a business as well as a profession, so the conversation about cost is part of the job.\"],[\"chef\",\"A chef's day is prep in the morning and service in the evening: deliveries, stocks, sauces, mise en place, then the rush where every plate is timed against a table's order. Split shifts and 50 to 60 hour weeks are normal in restaurants, and the kitchen is hot, loud and close. The satisfaction is at the pass when it all lands at once.\"],[\"waiter\",\"A waiter's day is sections and covers: taking orders, carrying, serving, clearing, and knowing the menu well enough to answer a question about an allergy. Shifts are split around lunch and dinner, the pay is minimum wage plus tips, and the job is on your feet for eight hours. Good service is mostly reading the table and being there when needed.\"],[\"hairdresser\",\"A hairdresser's day is a column of appointments, each with a consultation and a finish: colour work in the morning, cuts in the afternoon, and regulars who expect the same result every six weeks. It is on your feet with your arms up, and the money depends on building a clientele who ask for you. Saturdays are the busiest day of the week.\"],[\"cleaner\",\"A cleaner's day is a list of rooms and a time limit: bathrooms, kitchens, floors, bins, and the parts a household forgets. Domestic cleaning is usually hourly and self-employed, office cleaning is an early or late shift, and hotel work is rooms at a set number of minutes each. It is physical, mostly invisible, and judged only when it is done badly.\"],[\"retail worker\",\"A retail worker's day is the shop floor and the till: deliveries and replenishment before opening, then serving, fitting rooms, stock checks and the queue at the end of the day. Hours are shifts across the week, often including weekends, and the pay is close to the minimum wage with staff discount. Most of the job is being pleasant to people who are in a hurry.\"],[\"postman\",\"A postman's day is sorting in the depot, then a walk or a van round of the same streets with a bag and a bundle of parcels. The round is fixed, the weather is not, and the walking is 10 to 15 miles on a foot round. It finishes when the round is done, and Christmas is the season everyone remembers.\"],[\"warehouse worker\",\"A warehouse worker's day is on a pick list: a scanner, a trolley, and the same aisles at a rate the system measures. Goods-in and loading is heavier, packing is quicker, and shifts run round the clock in the big sheds. It is standing, lifting and walking all day, and the target is the reason the job is tiring.\"],[\"accountant\",\"An accountant's day is client work against a deadline: bookkeeping, VAT returns, year-end accounts and tax, with January and the filing dates setting the rhythm. Practice accountants see a dozen small businesses a week; in-house ones close the month and explain it to managers. The work is detail and software, and the busy season is genuinely busy.\"],[\"solicitor\",\"A solicitor's day is files and clients: reading, advice, letters, negotiation, and time recorded in six-minute units. Conveyancing is volume, family and crime are court dates, and commercial work is documents. The pressure is deadlines and other people's money, and most of the job is writing clearly about what should happen next.\"],[\"software developer\",\"A developer's day is a stand-up, then a task: reading code someone else wrote, writing a small piece of it, testing it, then reviewing someone else's change. The hard part is understanding the system, not typing, and most days include one meeting and one surprise. It is desk work, and it can be done from home more than most jobs.\"],[\"engineer\",\"An engineer's day depends which engineer: a design engineer works in CAD and calculations, a site engineer sets out and checks work against drawings, and a maintenance engineer is called when a machine stops. All of them are solving a problem with a constraint, and the paperwork is the record that the thing is safe.\"],[\"journalist\",\"A journalist's day is the diary, the calls and the deadline: find the story, check it with two sources, write it to length, and file before the page closes. Local reporting is council meetings and court lists; newsroom work is a running feed. The hours are unpredictable and the pay is low at the start.\"],[\"civil servant\",\"A civil servant's day is policy, casework or a service: reading, drafting, briefing a minister or a manager, and answering correspondence to a deadline. The work is steady, the process is the point, and the hours are usually 37.5 a week with flexitime. Change of government changes the priorities, not the building.\"],[\"banker\",\"A banker's day is clients who need money: reading accounts, structuring a loan, credit papers and meetings. High street banking is branch appointments and mortgages; investment banking is deals and very long hours. The pay is high at the top and ordinary at the counter, and the two jobs have almost nothing in common.\"]]}")?;
