let __v_5320 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"nurse\",\"A nurse in the NHS starts on band 5, about £30,000, and goes up to about £36,500 with experience; a specialist or ward manager is £37,000 to £45,000. London adds about £5,000, and nights, weekends and overtime are on top of the basic pay.\"],[\"care worker\",\"£12 to £13.50 an hour, about £23,000 to £26,000 full time; night shifts and sleep-ins add to that, and a senior care assistant gets £26,000 to £28,000. Experience and a level 3 qualification are what move it up.\"],[\"midwife\",\"Band 5 from about £30,000 to £36,500, and a midwife with experience on band 6 is £37,000 to £44,000. Unsocial hours add about 20% on top, so the take-home is higher than the band suggests.\"],[\"paramedic\",\"Band 5, so about £30,000 to £36,500, with a specialist paramedic on band 6 up to about £44,000. Shift allowance is on top and overtime is usually there for the taking.\"],[\"doctor\",\"A foundation doctor starts at about £38,000 and is on about £44,000 in the second year; a GP or a consultant is £70,000 to £130,000 depending on sessions, years and private work. The overtime and unsocial hours are already in those figures.\"],[\"dentist\",\"An NHS dentist is paid per item of treatment, roughly £30,000 to £65,000 a year, and a practice owner doing private work makes £80,000 to £150,000. What a patient pays for a check-up is a different number and a different question.\"],[\"pharmacist\",\"£40,000 to £50,000 in a community pharmacy, £45,000 to £55,000 in a hospital, and locum shifts at £30 to £45 an hour. A superintendent or a pharmacy owner earns more.\"],[\"vet\",\"£36,000 to £50,000 for a small animal vet with a few years in, and £60,000 upwards with a certificate or a partnership. Out-of-hours cover is paid on top and a locum day is £300 to £450.\"],[\"social worker\",\"Newly qualified is about £32,000, and with two or three years in it is £34,000 to £44,000; a senior practitioner or team manager is £44,000 to £55,000. Agency social workers are paid by the hour, often £30 to £40.\"],[\"teacher\",\"A classroom teacher in England starts at £31,650 and the main scale runs to £43,600; the leadership scale is £49,000 to £120,000. London adds 5% to 10%, and Scotland and Wales have their own scales.\"],[\"teaching assistant\",\"About £23,000 to £26,000 full time, £12.50 to £15 an hour, and the pay is spread over 12 months because the job is term time only. A higher level teaching assistant with a specialism gets £27,000 to £30,000.\"],[\"police officer\",\"A constable starts at about £29,000 and reaches about £46,000 after seven years; a sergeant is £48,000 to £52,000. Overtime, night duty and London weighting are on top.\"],[\"firefighter\",\"About £30,000 as a trainee firefighter, around £42,000 once competent with the shift allowance included, and a crew manager is £45,000 upwards. The shifts are what the money is for.\"],[\"hgv driver\",\"£14 to £18 an hour, about £32,000 to £42,000 a year. Nights out, ADR and weekend or overtime shifts push a lorry driver past £45,000, and agency work pays more in the weeks nobody wants.\"],[\"delivery driver\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £25,000 to £30,000, and parcel firms that pay per drop pay well in December and badly in January. Your own van and fuel come out of it if you are a subcontractor.\"],[\"bus driver\",\"£13 to £16 an hour, about £28,000 to £34,000, with London a few thousand more. Split shifts and unsocial hours are the trade-off, and overtime is regular.\"],[\"train driver\",\"£45,000 to £65,000 as a qualified driver, and more on long-distance and freight work. Training pay is lower and the training takes nine to twelve months.\"],[\"taxi driver\",\"Self-employed, so it is takings minus the car: £400 to £700 a week coming in, which is about £25,000 to £35,000 a year before fuel, insurance, maintenance and licensing.\"],[\"chef\",\"£13 to £17 an hour, about £26,000 to £35,000; a head chef is £32,000 to £45,000, and hotels and fine dining pay more. The hours are long and the pay is per hour worked.\"],[\"waiter\",\"The minimum wage and up: £11.44 to £13 an hour, plus tips, which are often pooled and shared out. A head waiter or supervisor is £13 to £15 an hour.\"],[\"retail worker\",\"£12 to £13 an hour, about £23,000 to £26,000 full time. A supervisor or team leader is £26,000 to £30,000, and Sunday and bank holiday premiums make a difference to the year.\"],[\"warehouse worker\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £24,000 to £28,000. Night shifts pay 20% to 30% more and agency rates move with demand, so the same job pays differently a mile apart.\"],[\"factory worker\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, £24,000 to £29,000 full time, with 15% to 30% extra for nights and rotating shifts. Skilled machine setting and maintenance is where the money is.\"],[\"security guard\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £24,000 to £28,000, and the SIA licence is what makes you employable at all. Nights, weekends and site work pay more than a reception desk.\"],[\"postman\",\"£13 to £14 an hour, about £26,000 to £28,000, with driving duties and Christmas overtime on top. A delivery office manager is £30,000 to £35,000.\"],[\"office worker\",\"£23,000 to £30,000 for an administrator, and an office manager is £30,000 to £38,000. London adds £3,000 to £5,000, and the sectors that pay best are finance and law.\"],[\"accountant\",\"£35,000 to £55,000 once qualified, and about £38,000 newly qualified in practice. A finance manager in industry is £50,000 to £65,000, and London and the big firms pay more again.\"],[\"solicitor\",\"About £40,000 newly qualified outside London, and £60,000 to £90,000 with five years in. A City firm pays £100,000 and up from day one, for hours to match.\"],[\"software developer\",\"£40,000 to £65,000 with a few years in, and senior or lead roles £70,000 to £100,000. Contract work is £400 to £600 a day with no holiday pay, which is the trade.\"],[\"engineer\",\"£28,000 to £35,000 starting, £35,000 to £50,000 chartered, and £50,000 upwards for a principal or a specialist. Site, offshore and defence work pays a premium over an office desk.\"],[\"architect\",\"£32,000 to £40,000 newly qualified, £45,000 to £60,000 with a decade in, and a partner or a practice owner more than that. The years of low pay before qualification are the part people do not expect.\"],[\"journalist\",\"£24,000 to £32,000 at a local paper or a trade title, and £35,000 to £50,000 in a national newsroom. Freelance is paid per word and the money arrives late.\"],[\"librarian\",\"£26,000 to £34,000, and a chartered or senior librarian is £35,000 to £45,000. Council library budgets are why the top of that range is thin.\"],[\"civil servant\",\"An administrative officer is £25,000 to £28,000, an executive officer £28,000 to £34,000, and a higher executive officer £34,000 to £40,000. The London pay bands add £4,000 to £5,000.\"],[\"banker\",\"Branch and mortgage staff are £25,000 to £35,000 plus a small bonus; an investment banking analyst is £60,000 to £100,000 with a bonus that can double it, for twice the hours.\"],[\"barrister\",\"£25,000 to £40,000 in the first years of practice, £60,000 to £150,000 with a decade in, and a handful earn far more. Chambers rent, insurance and no sick pay come out of it.\"],[\"mechanic\",\"£13 to £18 an hour, £27,000 to £36,000, and a master technician or a main dealer specialist is £35,000 to £45,000. Your own tools are part of the deal.\"],[\"plumber\",\"An employed plumber is £28,000 to £36,000. Self-employed, the takings are £40 to £80 an hour, which sounds like more until the van, tools, insurance, pension and quiet weeks are paid for - about £40,000 to £60,000 a year.\"],[\"electrician\",\"An employed electrician is £30,000 to £38,000, and self-employed takings are £40 to £70 an hour before the van, the test gear, the insurance and the certification. A commercial or industrial spark earns more than a domestic one.\"],[\"hairdresser\",\"£12 to £15 an hour employed, about £23,000 to £28,000, plus tips. Self-employed in a chair you rent, the takings are the cut price less the rent, so £25,000 to £40,000 is the usual spread.\"],[\"bricklayer\",\"£15 to £20 an hour employed, £30,000 to £40,000, and piecework on a site can pay more in a good week. Rain and winter are the unpaid part.\"],[\"carpenter\",\"£15 to £20 an hour employed, about £30,000 to £40,000, and a bench joiner or a site carpenter with their own tools and van can clear £45,000 on price work.\"],[\"painter and decorator\",\"£14 to £18 an hour employed, £28,000 to £36,000, and self-employed day rates of £150 to £250. The preparation nobody sees is what the day rate is really for.\"],[\"builder\",\"A site manager is £40,000 to £55,000, a general builder running small jobs £35,000 to £50,000, and a self-employed builder's profit depends on the jobs they price well.\"],[\"roofer\",\"£14 to £19 an hour employed, £28,000 to £38,000, and a self-employed roofer's day rate is £180 to £280. Bad weather weeks are unpaid, which is why the day rate is high.\"],[\"plasterer\",\"£15 to £20 an hour employed, £30,000 to £40,000, and pricework on a new build is £3 to £5 a square metre. A good plasterer's day rate is £200 to £250.\"],[\"nursery nurse\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £22,000 to £25,000 full time, and a level 3 childcare qualification is what gets you the job. A room leader is £25,000 to £28,000 and a nursery manager £30,000 to £35,000.\"],[\"kitchen porter\",\"£11.50 to £13 an hour, about £21,000 to £24,000 full time, and it is usually the way into a kitchen. A commis chef with a year in is £22,000 to £25,000.\"],[\"bar staff\",\"The minimum wage and up, £11.44 to £13 an hour, plus tips and often a meal on shift. A shift supervisor or bar manager is £13 to £16 an hour, and the late finishes are part of the job.\"],[\"refuse collector\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £24,000 to £28,000, and the early starts are the trade. The driver of the round, or a team leader, is £28,000 to £32,000.\"],[\"receptionist\",\"£12 to £14 an hour, about £23,000 to £26,000 full time. A medical or dental receptionist is similar, and a hotel reception manager is £28,000 to £32,000.\"],[\"soldier\",\"£25,000 to £30,000 as a new soldier, with food and accommodation on top, and £35,000 to £45,000 as a sergeant with a few years in. Trade and specialist pay is extra, and the pension is the real perk.\"],[\"farmer\",\"It depends on the farm and the year: an employed farm worker is £12 to £14 an hour, about £25,000 to £29,000, and a farm manager £35,000 to £50,000. The owner's income is the profit, which can be nothing.\"]]}")?;
